\title{Edge-Spectral Supersaturation for Triangles via Trace Bridges}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We prove an edge-spectral supersaturation bound for triangles in finite simple graphs, phrased entirely in terms of a graph's own combinatorial invariants. Let $G$ be a finite simple graph with $m$ edges and $t$ triangles, let $A$ be its real adjacency matrix, and let $\lambda$ be an eigenvalue of $A$ that dominates the spectrum in absolute value (the Perron--Frobenius eigenvalue for a genuine graph). Writing $q = \lambda^2 - m$ for the \emph{spectral excess}, we establish $\lambda\,q \le 3t$. Two corollaries follow immediately: a $\sqrt{m}$-scaled form $\sqrt{m}\,q \le 3t$ valid whenever $q \ge 0$, matching the shape of the conjectured sharp bound $t \gtrsim q\sqrt{m}$ with explicit constant $\tfrac13$; and Nosal's inequality $\lambda^2 \le m$ for triangle-free graphs as the boundary case $t = 0$. The engine of the proof is a pair of \emph{trace bridges} connecting linear algebra to extremal combinatorics: $\tr(A^2) = 2m$ and $\tr(A^3) = 6t$. The second, which converts a count of closed length-three walks into a triangle count, rests on the elementary fact that a three-element set admits exactly $3! = 6$ orderings. We combine these with a self-contained spectral supersaturation inequality derived from a single pointwise cubic estimate, and certify the identities on the complete graph $K_3$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Extremal graph theory studies how a global constraint on a graph forces the presence---indeed the \emph{abundance}---of local substructures. The paradigm is \textbf{supersaturation}: once a graph exceeds the threshold at which a forbidden subgraph becomes unavoidable, that subgraph appears not once but in numbers growing with the excess. For triangles the classical thresholds are stated in terms of edge count (Tur\'an, Rademacher) or the spectral radius (Nosal, Nikiforov).

This paper develops the \emph{spectral} route in a form that keeps all quantities graph-intrinsic. The central object is the adjacency matrix $A = A(G)$, a real symmetric $n \times n$ matrix, and its eigenvalues. Two features matter: the second and third \emph{power sums} $\sum_i \mu_i^2$ and $\sum_i \mu_i^3$ equal twice the edge count and six times the triangle count respectively; and the largest eigenvalue $\lambda$ dominates the whole spectrum in modulus for a non-negative symmetric matrix.

Our main theorem states that a graph whose squared spectral radius $\lambda^2$ overshoots its edge count $m$ by an amount $q$ must contain at least $\tfrac13\lambda q$ triangles. This encodes a supersaturation phenomenon, Nosal's inequality as the case $t = 0$, and the shape of the conjectured sharp bound $t \gtrsim q\sqrt m$. The novelty is a fully self-contained derivation that isolates the two trace bridges and the combinatorial heart---the $3! = 6$ ordering count of a triangle's corners---as standalone components.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item A spectral supersaturation inequality for real symmetric matrices (Theorem~\ref{thm:matspsat}).
  \item The edge bridge $\tr(A^2) = 2m$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:edgebridge}).
  \item The triangle bridge $\tr(A^3) = 6t$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:tribridge}), built on the ordering lemma (Theorem~\ref{thm:ordering}).
  \item Graph supersaturation $\lambda q \le 3t$ and its $\sqrt m$ form (Theorems~\ref{thm:main} and~\ref{thm:sqrt}).
  \item Nosal's inequality as a corollary (Theorem~\ref{thm:nosal}).
  \item A concrete certification on $K_3$ (Section~\ref{sec:k3}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions and setup}

Throughout, $V$ is a finite vertex set, $|V| = n$, and $G$ is a finite simple graph on $V$: an irreflexive symmetric adjacency relation, written $u \sim v$.

\begin{definition}[Adjacency matrix]
The adjacency matrix $A = A(G)$ is the $n \times n$ real matrix with $A_{uv} = 1$ if $u \sim v$ and $A_{uv} = 0$ otherwise. As $G$ is simple, $A$ is symmetric with zero diagonal, hence a real symmetric matrix with real eigenvalues $\mu_1, \dots, \mu_n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Spectral domination]
We fix a distinguished eigenvalue $\lambda = \mu_j$ and impose $|\mu_i| \le \lambda$ for all $i$. For a genuine graph this holds with $\lambda$ the Perron--Frobenius eigenvalue, since $A$ is entrywise non-negative and symmetric.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Invariants]
Let $m$ be the number of edges of $G$ and $t$ the number of triangles ($3$-cliques). The \emph{spectral excess} is $q = \lambda^2 - m$.
\end{definition}

We use the standard walk identity: $(A^k)_{uv}$ counts walks of length $k$ from $u$ to $v$; on the diagonal it counts closed walks.

\section{The spectral supersaturation inequality}

\begin{lemma}[Cubic domination]\label{lem:cube}
For real $\mu, \lambda$ with $|\mu| \le \lambda$, we have $-\lambda\mu^2 \le \mu^3$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $|\mu| \le \lambda$ we get $\mu + \lambda \ge 0$, and $\mu^2 \ge 0$, so $\mu^3 + \lambda\mu^2 = \mu^2(\mu+\lambda) \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Eigenvalue supersaturation]\label{thm:eigsat}
Let $\mu_1, \dots, \mu_n \in \R$, let $\lambda = \mu_j$ for some $j$, and suppose $|\mu_i| \le \lambda$ for all $i$. Then
\[ 2\lambda^3 - \lambda\sum_i \mu_i^2 \;\le\; \sum_i \mu_i^3. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cube} each summand $\mu_i^3 + \lambda\mu_i^2 \ge 0$, so the sum dominates its $j$-th term:
\[ \mu_j^3 + \lambda\mu_j^2 \le \sum_i(\mu_i^3 + \lambda\mu_i^2) = \sum_i\mu_i^3 + \lambda\sum_i\mu_i^2. \]
Since $\mu_j = \lambda$, the left side is $\lambda^3 + \lambda^3 = 2\lambda^3$; rearrange.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Trace--power-sum identity]\label{lem:trace}
For a real symmetric matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\mu_1, \dots, \mu_n$ and every $k \in \N$, $\tr(A^k) = \sum_i \mu_i^k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By the spectral theorem $A = QDQ^\top$ with $D = \mathrm{diag}(\mu_i)$ and $Q^\top Q = I$, so $A^k = QD^kQ^\top$ with $D^k = \mathrm{diag}(\mu_i^k)$. By cyclicity of the trace, $\tr(A^k) = \tr(D^k) = \sum_i \mu_i^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Matrix spectral supersaturation]\label{thm:matspsat}
Let $A$ be a real symmetric matrix with distinguished eigenvalue $\lambda = \mu_j$ dominating the spectrum in modulus. Then $2\lambda^3 - \lambda\,\tr(A^2) \le \tr(A^3)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:eigsat} to the eigenvalues and rewrite the power sums as traces via Lemma~\ref{lem:trace}.
\end{proof}

\section{The trace bridges}

\begin{theorem}[Edge bridge]\label{thm:edgebridge}
For a finite simple graph $G$ with real adjacency matrix $A$ and $m$ edges, $\tr(A^2) = 2m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(A^2)_{uu} = \sum_v A_{uv}^2 = \sum_v A_{uv} = \deg(u)$ since entries are $0/1$. Hence $\tr(A^2) = \sum_u \deg(u) = 2m$ by the handshake lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ordered representatives of a $3$-set]\label{thm:ordering}
Let $s$ be a set of exactly three elements. The number of ordered triples $(x,y,z)$ with $\{x,y,z\} = s$ is $6$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $s = \{a,b,c\}$ with $a,b,c$ pairwise distinct. An ordered triple with underlying set $s$ is exactly a permutation of $(a,b,c)$; there are $3! = 6$, and they are pairwise distinct.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Triangle bridge]\label{thm:tribridge}
For a finite simple graph $G$ with real adjacency matrix $A$ and $t$ triangles, $\tr(A^3) = 6t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(A^3)_{uu}$ counts closed length-three walks $u \to v \to w \to u$, so
\[ \tr(A^3) = \#\{(u,v,w) : u\sim v,\ v\sim w,\ w\sim u\}. \]
Irreflexivity forces $u,v,w$ pairwise distinct, so each such \emph{cyclic triple} has underlying set a triangle. Partitioning cyclic triples by their triangle, Theorem~\ref{thm:ordering} shows each triangle contributes exactly $6$ (all six orderings are cyclic since all three edges are present). Hence $\tr(A^3) = 6t$.
\end{proof}

\section{Graph-theoretic supersaturation}

Fix $G$ with adjacency matrix $A$, spectrum-dominating eigenvalue $\lambda = \mu_j$, edge count $m$, triangle count $t$, and $q = \lambda^2 - m$.

\begin{theorem}[Edge-spectral triangle supersaturation]\label{thm:main}
$\lambda\,q \le 3t.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:matspsat}, $2\lambda^3 - \lambda\tr(A^2) \le \tr(A^3)$. Substituting $\tr(A^2) = 2m$ and $\tr(A^3) = 6t$ gives $2\lambda^3 - 2\lambda m \le 6t$, i.e. $2\lambda(\lambda^2 - m) \le 6t$, i.e. $\lambda q \le 3t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\sqrt m$ scaling]\label{thm:sqrt}
If $q \ge 0$ then $\sqrt m\,q \le 3t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Domination gives $\lambda \ge 0$. From $q \ge 0$ and $\lambda^2 = m + q$ we get $\lambda \ge \sqrt m$, so $\sqrt m\,q \le \lambda q \le 3t$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nosal's inequality]\label{thm:nosal}
If $G$ is triangle-free ($t = 0$) then $\lambda^2 \le m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} gives $\lambda q \le 0$ with $\lambda \ge 0$. If $\lambda > 0$ then $q \le 0$, i.e. $\lambda^2 \le m$; if $\lambda = 0$ the inequality is trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} strengthens Nosal's bound: crossing $\lambda = \sqrt m$ forces not a single triangle but at least $\tfrac13\lambda(\lambda^2 - m)$ of them.
\end{remark}

\section{A concrete certification: the triangle $K_3$}\label{sec:k3}

For $G = K_3$ we have $m = 3$, $t = 1$, and eigenvalues $\mu_1 = 2$, $\mu_2 = \mu_3 = -1$, so $\lambda = 2$.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Edge bridge: $\tr(A^2) = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6 = 2m$.
  \item Triangle bridge: $\tr(A^3) = 8 - 1 - 1 = 6 = 6t$.
  \item Supersaturation: $q = 4 - 3 = 1$, so $\lambda q = 2 \le 3 = 3t$.
\end{itemize}
This certifies the bridges are non-vacuous and the bound is tight-in-shape on the extremal small case.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Trace-based triangle count.} Given $A$, compute $t = \tfrac16\tr(A^3)$ and $m = \tfrac12\tr(A^2)$ by matrix multiplication; complexity $O(n^\omega)$.

\textbf{Spectral supersaturation certificate.} Compute the eigenvalues, set $\lambda = \max_i|\mu_i|$, $m = \tfrac12\sum\mu_i^2$, $t = \tfrac16\sum\mu_i^3$, $q = \lambda^2 - m$, and verify $\lambda q \le 3t$ (and $\sqrt m\,q \le 3t$ when $q \ge 0$).

\section{Discussion and future work}

The argument is modular: an abstract eigenvalue inequality, two trace bridges, and their combination. Natural extensions include: discharging the Perron--Frobenius domination hypothesis for $A(G)$ to make the bounds unconditional; sharpening the constant from $\tfrac13$ toward the conjectured $1$ by controlling the negative spectrum; extending trace bridges $\tr(A^k) = c_k\cdot\#(k\text{-cliques})$ to higher cliques via closed $k$-walks; and repackaging the triangle bridge abstractly via closed-walk counting for reuse.

\section{Conclusion}

Threading a single elementary inequality through two trace identities yields a clean, self-contained edge-spectral supersaturation bound $\lambda q \le 3t$ for triangles, recovering Nosal's inequality as its boundary case and the conjectured $q\sqrt m$ scaling with an explicit constant. The trace is the translator between algebra and combinatorics, and the six orderings of a triangle are its dictionary's key entry.

